      "lang": "en",
      "notes": [
         {
            "category": "description",
            "text": "HTTP::Headers::ActionPack versions before 0.10 for Perl allow CPU exhaustion via Accept-Language ranges matched as regular expressions.\n\n_language_match interpolates each Accept-Language range into the pattern `/^$range\\-/i` without escaping it, so regular expression syntax in the header reaches the regex engine. A 12-byte range such as `(a?|b?){26}z` backtracks for seconds, and each increment of the quantifier doubles the time. An invalid range such as `(` fails to compile.\n\nA remote attacker can exhaust CPU, or make choose_language die, in any application that passes an untrusted Accept-Language header to it.",
            "title": "Vulnerability Description"
         }
      ],
      "publisher": {
